# ===========================================================================
# STAGE #2G - Closing the mutation survivors
# ===========================================================================
#
# Stage 24 re-verified as sound, then the two real survivors closed and the third explained.
#
# This notebook is COMPLETE IN ITSELF. It does not depend on, and does not
# refer you to, any other notebook. Everything needed to reproduce, run,
# view and understand this stage is written out below.
#
# WHAT THIS STAGE WAS ABOUT
#
# It opened with a question: does the earlier Bessel stage need redoing?
#
# ANSWER: NO, AND THE EVIDENCE IS NOT AN OPINION. All three of that stage's
# fixes are independently corroborated by a mutation probe built two stages
# LATER, by a different method - deliberately breaking the Debye branch
# discriminator, the sector guard and the validity threshold each causes the
# suite to fail. Its three regression tests pass. A stage whose conclusions
# can be re-derived by a tool written afterwards is as verified as this
# project gets. Redoing sound work would have cost a stage and found nothing.
#
# What it HAD left was one thing in the same module that nothing asserted.
#
# SURVIVOR 1 - THE ESTIMATE FLOOR. CLOSED. A constant exists because an
# error estimate that bottoms out too low wins every comparison in the
# route selector. The first attempt to pin it used the J-Y Wronskian at the
# point the code's own comment cites - and the residual there is 4.11e-17,
# three decades below the 3.2e-14 the comment records. THE INSTRUMENT WAS
# WRONG, not the constant: a Wronskian can be small while the values are
# not. The floor actually binds where optimal truncation reports EXACTLY
# ZERO, which happens at nu = 1/2 where the 1/z series TERMINATES. Pinned
# against the closed form J_(1/2)(z) = sqrt(2/(pi z)) sin z.
#
# SURVIVOR 2 - THE ABSORBER'S CELL RESOLUTION. CLOSED. The convergence test
# built its own geometry to measure the 1/n^2 law, so the LAW was pinned and
# the SHIPPED CONSTANT was not. A test can verify the right property of the
# wrong object.
#
# SURVIVOR 3 - THE ZENO THRESHOLD. STILL OPEN, AND NOW UNDERSTOOD. A corner
# impact was written specifically to exercise it and did not: the collision
# solver handles every flagged pair in ONE event, so simultaneous contacts
# produce one event carrying two contacts, not two events, and the burst
# counter never rises.
#
# The corner test was kept anyway - an elastic corner reflection must
# preserve speed and reverse both velocity components, and nothing asserted
# that before.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOW TO RUN THIS NOTEBOOK
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Everything below is posim notebook source. Lines beginning with '#' are
# comments and are ignored by the interpreter; every other line is a command
# that will be executed in order.
#
# From the repository root (the directory holding Cargo.toml), run ONE of:
#
#     cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook StageNbks/Stage_2G.posim
#     cargo run -p posim --release -- --script   StageNbks/Stage_2G.posim
#
#   --notebook  prints an In[n]:= / Out[n]= transcript, like a Mathematica
#               notebook. This is the mode to use when reading along.
#   --script    prints the same transcript; the two differ only in how the
#               prompt lines are laid out. Either is reproducible.
#
# To work through it by hand instead, start the interactive interpreter:
#
#     cargo run -p posim --release
#
# and paste the command lines one at a time. Type HELP for the full command
# language, and %quit to leave.
#
# First run will compile the workspace (a few minutes). Later runs are
# immediate. If 'cargo' is not found, install Rust from https://rustup.rs .
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE BROWSER GUI - THIS NOTEBOOK OPENS IT FOR YOU
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The command lines at the end of this notebook build the bodies, open the
# scene window and START it, so running the notebook gives you a moving
# picture with no further action.
#
# WHAT HAPPENS WHEN YOU RUN IT:
#
#   'scene create' prints an address such as
#
#       scene window created: http://127.0.0.1:41234/
#
#   and normally opens it in your default browser. The port is chosen
#   freely each run. IF NO WINDOW APPEARED, copy that printed address into
#   any browser - it is plain HTML/CSS/JavaScript over a websocket and needs
#   nothing installed. To pin the port instead, edit the line to read
#   'scene create 8080' and the address is always http://127.0.0.1:8080/ .
#
#   'scene start' then begins the evolution, so the picture is already
#   moving when you look at it.
#
# EVERY BUTTON IN THE WINDOW, AND ITS TYPED EQUIVALENT:
#
#   Start    -> scene start          begin / resume evolving
#   Pause    -> scene pause          freeze in place, keep the window live
#   Stop     -> scene stop           halt the evolution
#   Reverse  -> scene reverse        play backwards through the snapshot ring.
#                                     NOTE: this needs forward history to
#                                     exist first. Immediately after loading
#                                     it answers "nothing to reverse — no
#                                     forward history recorded yet"; let the
#                                     scene RUN for a few seconds and it
#                                     works. That is not a fault: there is
#                                     nothing recorded to play backwards.
#   step     -> the arrow keys advance or retreat one frame
#   zoom     -> scene zoom in | out | <factor>, or the mouse wheel, or +/-
#   orbit    -> drag with the left mouse button
#   pan      -> shift-drag, or the arrow keys
#
#   Typed anywhere at the In[n]:= prompt while the window is open:
#     scene set_time_step <dt>   simulated seconds per frame (see below)
#     scene refresh              re-read the body list after adding bodies
#     scene status               report what the window is doing
#     scene close                shut the window and free the port
#
#   HELP lists every scene command with its arguments.
#
# THE TIME STEP MATTERS. 'scene set_time_step' controls how much simulated
# time each frame advances. Too large and fast bodies jump past each other
# between frames; the value used below was chosen for this scenario.
#
# TROUBLESHOOTING, precisely:
#   * 'no scene window - run SCENE CREATE first' means the window was closed
#     (or never opened): type 'scene create' again.
#   * A window showing '0 entities' means the bodies were not built - re-run
#     the 'new ...' lines, then 'scene refresh'.
#   * If the port is in use: 'scene close', then 'scene create <other port>'.
#   * The window is bound to 127.0.0.1 and is not reachable from another
#     machine, by design.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ANIMATING THIS STAGE - PARTLY POSSIBLE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# PART 2 of the scenario above (the corner impact) IS a rigid-body scene and
# animates properly. PARTS 1 and 3 cannot be animated: a Bessel function has
# no time coordinate and no position, and a shell harness has no state that
# evolves - the scene window draws simulator bodies, and neither creates one.
#
# COMPLETE PROCEDURE for the corner impact:
#
#   1. cargo run -p posim --release
#
#   2. Paste the PART 2 lines above, down to and including the
#      'new sphere { ... }' line. Do not run 'run' yet.
#
#   3. scene create
#      posim replies with an address such as
#          scene window created: http://127.0.0.1:41234/
#      and normally opens it. If not, paste that address into any browser.
#      To pin the port:  scene create 8080  ->  http://127.0.0.1:8080/
#
#   4. scene set_time_step 0.002
#      scene zoom 2
#      scene start
#
#   5. Watch the ball come in diagonally, strike both slabs at the same
#      instant, and leave along the reversed diagonal at the same speed.
#      Drag to orbit, scroll to zoom, shift-drag or arrows to pan.
#      'energy' at the prompt reads the invariant while it plays.
#
#   6. scene close   when finished.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RECREATING THE STAGE SCENARIO
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# PART 1 - the special-function survivor, reproducible in posim.
#
# The estimate floor binds at nu = 1/2, where the 1/z Hankel series
# TERMINATES and optimal truncation would report an error of exactly zero.
# The closed form is elementary there:
#
#     J_(1/2)(z) = sqrt(2/(pi z)) sin z
#
# Compute both and compare.
bessel_j_nu(0.5, 18.0)
sqrt(2 / (3.14159265358979 * 18.0)) * sin(18.0)

bessel_j_nu(0.5, 40.0)
sqrt(2 / (3.14159265358979 * 40.0)) * sin(40.0)

# And the companion closed form, Y_(1/2)(z) = -sqrt(2/(pi z)) cos z.
bessel_y_nu(0.5, 18.0)
-sqrt(2 / (3.14159265358979 * 18.0)) * cos(18.0)

# PART 2 - the collision survivor, reproducible in posim. A ball driven
# into the corner where two static slabs meet gives two contacts at the
# same instant. An elastic corner reflection must preserve the speed and
# reverse both components.
reset
set system.g_constant = 0
new cuboid { mass = 1, half_extents = [0.5, 4, 4], position = [-1.5, 0, 0] }
set obj0.inverse_mass = 0
new cuboid { mass = 1, half_extents = [4, 0.5, 4], position = [0, -1.5, 0] }
set obj1.inverse_mass = 0
new sphere { mass = 1, radius = 0.25, position = [2, 2, 0], velocity = [-3, -3, 0] }
energy
run 2 steps 20
energy
get obj2.velocity

# PART 3 - the mutation probe itself, from a normal shell at the repo root:
#
#     bash scripts/mutation_probe.sh
#     bash scripts/mutation_probe.sh debye     # just the Stage 24 artifacts
#
# Expect the three Debye mutations to be CAUGHT: that is the re-verification
# described above.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOW DISPLAY THE CORNER IMPACT. The bodies from PART 2 are still loaded, so
# these lines open the window and start it running.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
scene create
scene set_time_step 0.002
scene zoom 2
scene start
scene status

# ===========================================================================
# END OF STAGE #2G NOTEBOOK
# ===========================================================================
